summary.text:
Good. Now the fix in `watch_for_stop.ts`. Let me write it.

Current:
```ts
export function stopRequested(comments: StopComment[], since: Date): boolean {
  return comments.some((c) => {
    // The notice quoting a removed comment carries that comment verbatim: a stop tag
    // inside it is the DELETED author's words, and the notice's own line says the
    // comment will not be acted on. Acting on the quotation would do exactly that --
    // stop a run the notice told somebody would keep going. See `isQuotation`.
    if (isQuotation(c.body ?? "")) return false;
    if (!STOP_TAG.has(c.body ?? "")) return false;
```

New:
```ts
export function stopRequested(comments: StopComment[], since: Date): boolean {
  return comments.some((c) => {
    // A stop tag counts only where it is the machinery speaking. A notice that preserves
    // a removed comment carries that comment verbatim, and a stop tag inside the preserved
    // text is the DELETED author's words on a notice whose own line says the comment will
    // not be acted on -- acting on it would stop a run the notice told somebody would keep
    // going. Preserved text is always fenced (`lib/removed-comment.ts`), so prose alone is
    // the machinery's own speech. Not `isQuotation`: a stop RECEIPT quotes the comment too,
    // and its stop tag is in its prose, which is exactly the request polled for (#297).
    if (!STOP_TAG.has(proseOnly(c.body ?? ""))) return false;
```

Now update the import: remove `isQuotation`, add `proseOnly`.

Then update `QUOTES_TAG` doc and `isQuotation` doc: "written by exactly one writer -- the guard" is now false. Update to say two writers: the guard and the stop receipt. And the semantic: the tag marks that the body QUOTES a person's words (inside a fence); readers that must tell machinery speech from a person's words ask `proseOnly` for the fenced part... hmm. Let me think about the accurate statement.

Actually let me reconsider: is `isQuotation` still the right predicate for the other readers? Those readers scan for tags like agent, ended, ci-retry, dispatch. The quotation tag on the stop receipt would make them skip the receipt — but the receipt carries none of those tags in prose, so behaviour is unchanged. So keeping `isQuotation` in those readers is fine and conservative.

But the doc for `QUOTES_TAG` says "Written by the mid-run guard ... on the notice that replaces a deleted comment". Now also written by request_stop.ts on the receipt. Update.

And `isQuotation` doc: "The tag is written by exactly one writer -- the guard -- so on a raw body it marks a quotation. A person writing the same tag..." Need to update to two writers, and note that for the stop poll, the fenced-ness matters (proseOnly).

Let me be careful not to bloat. The instruction: comments say why not, not history. I'll make targeted updates.

Now the tests.

1. `watch_for_stop.test.ts`: add a test that prose stop tag + fenced quotation is a stop (the receipt shape), and keep the existing one. Actually better to have request_stop.test.ts build the real receipt and feed it to stopRequested. Let's do both:

In `watch_for_stop.test.ts`, add:

```ts
  // A stop RECEIPT quotes the removed comment too, but its own tag is in its prose: the
  // quotation is what must be stepped over, not the whole body. The receipt for
  // `/stop` + `/reviewer` is this shape (see request_stop.test.ts), and reading it as a
  // quotation left the run going after the person had been told it would stop (#297).
  test("a stop tag in the notice's own prose is a request, whatever it quotes", () => {
    const receipt = [
      "<!-- atomaton:llm-context=exclude -->",
      "<!-- atomaton:quotes=77 -->",
      "<!-- atomaton:stop=requested -->",
      "Atomaton: stop requested.",
      "<details><summary>Your comment, as you wrote it</summary>",
      "",
      "```",
      "/stop",
      "/reviewer",
      "```",
      "",
      "</details>",
    ].join("\n");
    expect(stopRequested([{ body: receipt, created_at: "2026-09-06T12:00:30Z" }], since)).toBe(true);
  });
```

Hmm, but hardcoding the body duplicates the writer. Better: in `request_stop.test.ts`, import `stopRequested` and assert against the real `stopRequestedNotice` output. That's the true regression. Then in `watch_for_stop.test.ts`, a focused prose-vs-fence test is still valuable and doesn't need request_stop.

Let me do both but keep them lean. Actually, duplicating the receipt shape in watch_for_stop.test.ts risks drift. I'll instead add in watch_for_stop.test.ts a small test purely about the rule:

```ts
  // The rule is "the stop tag must be the machinery's own speech", not "the body must
  // not quote anybody": a notice whose tag is in its prose is a request even when it
  // quotes a comment underneath. The stop receipt is exactly that shape (#297).
  test("acts on a notice whose own prose carries the tag, despite a quotation", () => {
    const receipt = `${STOP_TAG.write("requested")}\n\n\`\`\`\n/stop\n/reviewer\n\`\`\``;
    expect(stopRequested([{ body: receipt, created_at: "2026-09-06T12:00:30Z" }], since)).toBe(true);
  });
```

Hmm, that doesn't include QUOTES_TAG. It should, to be the real shape. Add `QUOTES_TAG.write(77)`. And assert the quoted stop tag isn't counted: `STOP_TAG.write` inside the fence with no prose tag → false. That's basically the existing test 46.

Best approach: one test in watch_for_stop.test.ts using the real writer would require importing request_stop.ts. Is that acceptable? `request_stop.test.ts` already imports from request_stop.ts. Importing `stopRequestedNotice` in watch_for_stop.test.ts is fine, no cycle (request_stop.ts doesn't import watch_for_stop.ts). But it couples a reader's test to a writer's module. Actually that's exactly the regression the reviewer wants: "a test on `stopRequested` with the new receipt". So I'll put it in `request_stop.test.ts`:

```ts
  test("is still a stop the running job acts on", () => {
    const notice = stopRequestedNotice("octocat", true, [], extra);
    const since = new Date("2026-09-06T12:00:00Z");
    expect(stopRequested([{ body: notice, created_at: "2026-09-06T12:00:30Z" }], since)).toBe(true);
