Decision: 
- `watch_for_stop.test.ts`: add one test with the real receipt shape built by hand? No — instead, add import of `stopRequestedNotice`? No.

Simplest, cleanest: put the real-receipt regression in `request_stop.test.ts` (writer + reader together, no duplication), and in `watch_for_stop.test.ts` update the comment of the existing test to name the distinction and add nothing. Hmm, but then the reader's own test file doesn't pin "prose tag counts". The existing test at line 10 "finds a stop request posted after the run started" does pin prose tag counts (REQUEST has tag in prose, no fence). And line 46 pins fenced tag doesn't count. Together they pin the rule. Good — no new test needed in watch_for_stop.test.ts. But the comment at 42-45 could be updated to say "inside the quoted text" (fenced), which it already does. 

Actually one refinement: line 46's body has QUOTES_TAG and the stop tag only inside the fence. With the new implementation it passes. Good.

I'll add to `watch_for_stop.test.ts` a brief note in the line-46 test that the tag is inside the fence, which is what makes it a quotation — and that a receipt's prose tag is different (#297). Minimal edit, no new test. Hmm, but "a test on stopRequested with the new receipt is the regression this needs" — that's the request_stop.test.ts one. Good.

Now also check: `isQuotation` import removal from watch_for_stop.ts — is `isQuotation` still used elsewhere? Let me grep.

Also need to check other tests that might assert `watch_for_stop.ts` uses `isQuotation` (e.g. a contract test listing raw-body readers). Let me grep for `isQuotation`.

Also check whether there are tests asserting the receipt's QUOTES_TAG is present in a way that would now conflict.

